# secrets — never commit; .env.example carries the placeholders
.env

# derived — regenerate with `make dataset-materialize`
data/materialized/*
!data/materialized/README.md

# experiment output — commit what you promote, not every run
data/runs/*
!data/runs/README.md
# The ONE worked example that ships, so a fresh checkout sees a walked loop instead of an
# empty directory: sources -> dataset -> reference -> run -> baseline. It is also what keeps
# the tree self-consistent — data/baselines/ is committed and V6 requires each baseline's
# source run to exist, so a committed baseline must point at a committed run. Your own runs
# stay ignored by default; commit them if you want them, that is your call.
!data/runs/demo_v1_20260925T070923Z/
!data/runs/demo_v1_20260925T070923Z/**

# judge panel output
out/

.venv/
__pycache__/

# ── third-party corpus content: NEVER committed to this public repo ──────────
# CNN/DailyMail article text and its human-written highlights belong to the dataset's
# publishers, not to us. The example ships a DOWNLOAD RECIPE instead — see
# ../summarization-cnn-dailymail/fetch.py and its README — so anyone can reproduce the
# tree locally without us redistributing anything.
#
# The synthetic demo items (data/sources/item_0*.txt) are OURS and stay tracked; that is
# why these rules name the dataset directory rather than the whole of sources/.
# Convention for the next example: real corpora live in data/sources/<dataset_id>/ and
# get a line here.
# Globbed by slice size, not written out per size: `fetch.py --n 200` names its dataset
# cnn_dailymail_200, and a corpus whose exclusion depends on someone remembering to add a
# line is one forgotten line away from being redistributed.
data/sources/cnn_dailymail_*/
data/references/gold/cnn_dailymail_*/
data/references/silver/cnn_dailymail_*/

# AG News, same rule and a weaker licence than CNN/DailyMail's: the dataset card says
# `unknown` and the corpus's own description says non-commercial research use. We
# redistribute nothing -- examples/classification-ag-news/fetch.py downloads a slice to
# whoever runs it. These lines go in BEFORE the first fetch, not after: the window between
# downloading a third-party corpus and remembering to ignore it is the window in which it
# gets committed to a public repo.
data/sources/ag_news_*/
data/references/gold/ag_news_*/
data/references/silver/ag_news_*/

# DBpedia-14. Licence here is actually clean (CC-BY-SA 3.0 + GFDL, inherited from
# Wikipedia) -- unlike AG News -- and it is still not ours to redistribute. Same rule,
# same reason, same timing: these lines land before the first fetch.
data/sources/dbpedia_*/
data/references/gold/dbpedia_*/
data/references/silver/dbpedia_*/

# Few-NERD. CC BY-SA 4.0 -- the cleanest licence of the four corpora here -- and still
# not ours to redistribute. Same rule, same timing: in before the first fetch.
data/sources/few_nerd_*/
data/references/gold/few_nerd_*/
data/references/silver/few_nerd_*/

# Rescored runs are derived: regenerate with `make rescore`. Same reasoning as
# data/runs/ -- the originals are the record, these are a recomputation of them.
data/runs-rescored*/

# Any other EVAL_RUNS_DIR: smoke passes, timing probes, throwaway scopes. Ignored for the
# same reason, plus one the last sweep learned the hard way -- an untracked directory under
# the harness makes the tree DIRTY, and every run launched beside it records
# `harness.dirty: true` and cannot be reproduced from its own fingerprint.
data/runs-smoke*/
data/runs-tune*/
# GLOBBED, not enumerated, for the reason the corpus rules above are: a rule that needs
# someone to remember a new line is one forgotten line away from committing 240 run
# artifacts. `data/runs-tune/` was exactly that -- the comment above already said "any
# other EVAL_RUNS_DIR" and only `runs-smoke*` was actually listed.
data/runs-[a-z]*/
!data/runs/
# The superseded-runs README is the CONVENTION, not an artifact: runs whose
# instrument changed are moved there rather than deleted. The runs stay ignored.
!data/runs-superseded/README.md

# BEIR SciFact. CC BY-SA 4.0, and still not ours to redistribute. Same rule, same timing:
# in before the first fetch.
data/sources/scifact_*/
data/references/gold/scifact_*/
data/references/silver/scifact_*/

# ── the CORPUS, which is a new kind of thing in this tree ────────────────────
# Retrieval is the first task here whose answer is not computable from the item alone. An
# item is a QUERY; the documents it must find are a separate 5,183-abstract corpus that
# every arm indexes and no arm is scored on. It is not `sources/` (not one file per item),
# not `references/` (not ground truth), and not `materialized/` (not derived from our
# inputs) — so it gets its own directory, and the same never-redistribute rule.
#
# A corpus that is not fingerprinted is the worst silent failure available to this repo:
# two runs over the SAME queries against DIFFERENT corpora would otherwise look identical
# in every recorded field. `corpus_sha256` is in the arm fingerprint for that reason, and
# the manifest that carries it is the one tracked file here.
data/corpora/*
!data/corpora/README.md
